# Build context filter for deploy/fleet-manager/Dockerfile.
#
# BuildKit reads `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore` in preference to the context root's
# `.dockerignore`, which is what lets this repository have a context filter for the Fleet Manager
# image without imposing one on every other `docker build` run against the root.
#
# The context is the REPOSITORY ROOT, not this directory, because the image is built from source:
# the publish needs src/, the committed wwwroot and the solution-level props files, and it needs
# build/out — where the agent binaries the Fleet Manager serves are staged by the *other* build
# path. That last one is the reason build/out is conspicuously not excluded here despite being
# gitignored.

# Version control history. Large, and nothing in the build reads it.
.git
.gitattributes

# Intermediate and per-project output from the developer's own machine. Excluding these is
# correctness, not tidiness: a project.assets.json restored on Windows with no RID, copied into
# the build stage, makes `dotnet publish --runtime linux-x64` fail with NETSDK1047 "Assets file
# doesn't have a target for 'net10.0/linux-x64'" — the same collision build/build.sh documents
# from the other direction.
**/bin
**/obj
artifacts

# The GUI's source dependencies and its incremental cache. wwwroot is committed and is what the
# image serves; node_modules is hundreds of megabytes of things the image build never opens,
# because the publish runs with -p:FrameLinkBuildGui=false.
**/node_modules
**/.svelte-kit

# Harness run artifacts: screenshots, journal captures, console dumps.
tools/harness/runs
**/__pycache__

# Scratch and editor state.
.vs
.vscode
.idea
*.user

# Documentation and images. Nothing in the container renders them, and docs/ carries the guide
# screenshots, which are the single largest thing in the tree after .git.
docs
research
reference
*.md

# The v1 card image. 120 GB of it, measured 2026-08-24.
#
# This one is not tidiness and it is not an optimisation - without it `docker build` cannot
# complete at all, because the context is uploaded to the daemon before the first instruction
# runs. .gitignore already covers archive/, but Docker has never read .gitignore, and BuildKit
# reads THIS file in preference to a context-root .dockerignore, so a rule written anywhere
# else would not apply to this image. The directory was created after the last image build,
# which is why nothing noticed.
archive

# Intermediates of the *agent* build. build/out is deliberately in the context - it is where
# the agent binaries the Fleet Manager serves are staged - but only build/out/<rid>/ is ever
# read, by the staging loop in the Dockerfile. artifacts/ is 167 MB of MSBuild output
# including a 50 MB .o and a 16 MB .dbg, and publish/ is another 27 MB of the same ELF under
# a different name; both are uploaded on every image build and opened by none of it.
build/out/artifacts
build/out/publish
